[0.000, "o", "ities[]`, `present_entities[]`, or `dormant_entities[]` they appear in \\u2014 all three tiers carry a `status` field (`npc_data` is not sent to the model). If the NPC is absent from ALL THREE tiers, do not assume they are alive, present, or interactive based on narrative memory alone \\u2014 treat their status as unknown and avoid choices that require an interactive premise.\\n   - If `status` \\u2208 {`dead`, `captured`, `imprisoned`, `missing`, `resigned`, `departed`, `gone`}: the choice's premise cannot treat them as actively present or interactive unless the same turn writes a justified restoration (per `master_directive.md` \\u00a7\\\"Canonical NPC Status\\\" \\u2014 resurrection or mistaken-death reveal).\\n   - Examples: choice *\\\"negotiate with Lord Tywin\\\"* when his `entity_tracking.active_entities[]` entry has `status == \\\"dead\\\"` \\u2192 DROP; choice *\\\"recruit Ser Barristan\\\"* when `status == \\\"captured\\\"` without a release-this-turn state update \\u2192 DROP.\\n\\n   **Why**: covers the NPC-status-class canoni"]
