[0.000, "o", "ld:**\\n- `microsecond`: (integer 0-999999) Technical field for database uniqueness during think blocks. **This is NOT narrative time**\\u2014it exists purely to ensure each response has a distinct timestamp. When incrementing microseconds during a think block, the world remains frozen; only the technical timestamp changes.\\n\\n### \\ud83d\\udea8 MANDATORY TIME FIELDS\\n\\n**CRITICAL: When updating world_time, date fields (year, month, day) MUST be present. Time fields (hour, minute, second, microsecond, time_of_day) are optional but recommended.**\\n\\n| Field | Type | Required | Description |\\n|-------|------|----------|-------------|\\n| `year` | integer | **YES** | The year (e.g., 1492, 3641) |\\n| `month` | string/integer | **YES** | Month name or number (e.g., \\\"Mirtul\\\", 5) |\\n| `day` | integer | **YES** | Day of month (1-31) |\\n| `hour` | integer | **OPTIONAL** | Hour (0-23) |\\n| `minute` | integer | **OPTIONAL** | Minute (0-59) |\\n| `second` | integer | **OPTIONAL** | Second (0-59) |\\n| `microsecond` | integer "]
[0.000, "o", "| **OPTIONAL** | Microsecond (0-999999) |\\n| `time_of_day` | string | **OPTIONAL** | Lowercase period name (dawn/morning/midday/afternoon/evening/night/deep night) |\\n\\n**\\u274c INVALID (missing year/month/day):**\\n```json\\n{\\\"world_time\\\": {\\\"hour\\\": 8, \\\"minute\\\": 15, \\\"time_of_day\\\": \\\"morning\\\"}}\\n```\\n\\n**\\u2705 VALID (all fields present):**\\n```json\\n{\\\"world_time\\\": {\\\"year\\\": 3641, \\\"month\\\": \\\"Mirtul\\\", \\\"day\\\": 20, \\\"hour\\\": 8, \\\"minute\\\": 15, \\\"second\\\": 0, \\\"microsecond\\\": 0, \\\"time_of_day\\\": \\\"morning\\\"}}\\n```\\n\\n**RULE: Copy all time fields from the current state, then modify only what changes.** Never generate partial time objects.\\n\\n### Backward Time Travel (GOD MODE ONLY)\\n\\nTime can ONLY move backward when:\\n1. User input explicitly starts with \\\"GOD MODE:\\\"\\n2. AND the god mode command explicitly requests time manipulation with an explicit imperative command (e.g., \\\"GOD MODE: Set time to Mirtul 10 evening\\\", \\\"GOD MODE: Rewind to the flashback timestamp...\\\")\\n\\nA lore correction, questio"]
[0.000, "o", "n, or observation about a past event is NOT a time-manipulation request, even when the user is in GOD MODE. Persist that correction as lore/directives/core memories instead of moving `world_time` backward.\\n\\n**Example God Mode Time Reset:**\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"god_mode_response\\\": \\\"Time reset to Mirtul 10, evening as requested.\\\",\\n  \\\"state_updates\\\": {\\n    \\\"world_data\\\": {\\n      \\\"world_time\\\": {\\n        \\\"year\\\": 1492,\\n        \\\"month\\\": \\\"Mirtul\\\",\\n        \\\"day\\\": 10,\\n        \\\"hour\\\": 19,\\n        \\\"minute\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"second\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"microsecond\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"time_of_day\\\": \\\"evening\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Validation Rule\\n\\nBefore outputting any `state_updates` containing `world_time`, mentally verify:\\n1. Is the new timestamp > previous timestamp? \\u2705 Proceed\\n2. Is the new timestamp \\u2264 previous timestamp?\\n   - Is this a GOD MODE time manipulation request (explicit imperative command: \\\"set time to ...\\\", \\\"rewind to ...\\\", \\\"reset time to ...\\\")? \\u2705 Pr"]
[0.000, "o", "oceed with warning in god_mode_response. A lore correction or question about past events is NOT a time-manipulation request.\\n   - Is this normal gameplay? \\u274c **HALT** - Do not output backward time. Increment forward instead.\\n\\n**FORBIDDEN (unless GOD MODE):**\\n- Setting time to an earlier date/hour/minute than current state\\n- Replaying scenes at their original timestamp\\n- \\\"Resuming\\\" from an earlier point without god mode authorization\\n\\n## Core Memory Log\\n\\n**MANDATORY: You MUST append a core memory on EVERY response with narrative text.**\\n\\nCore memories are canonical continuity facts that must survive story-history\\ncompaction. Old middle story entries are deliberately dropped from future LLM\\ncontext; core memories are the substitute record future turns use when those\\nentries are gone. They are not scene summaries, prose flourishes, vibes, or\\nrecent narration. Treat existing core memories as higher-authority continuity\\nfacts: if older story context conflicts with a core memory, the core mem"]
[0.000, "o", "ory\\nwins.\\n\\nEvery story-mode response must evaluate what non-duplicate durable memory delta\\nthis turn created, confirmed, corrected, invalidated, sourced, constrained, or\\nmade operational. Append exactly one such delta whenever narrative text is\\npresent.\\n\\nBefore choosing the append entry, ask: **\\\"If the full story entry for this turn\\nis removed, what one fact must a future LLM know to preserve continuity?\\\"**\\nAppend that fact, not a recap of the scene.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"custom_campaign_state\\\": {\\\"core_memories\\\": {\\\"append\\\": \\\"1-sentence canonical continuity fact\\\"}}}\\n```\\n\\n**Update (replace an existing memory):**\\n```json\\n{\\\"custom_campaign_state\\\": {\\\"core_memories\\\": {\\\"update\\\": {\\\"replace\\\": \\\"unique substring of existing memory\\\", \\\"with\\\": \\\"replacement memory text\\\"}}}}\\n```\\n\\nUse `update` when this turn corrects, refines, or supersedes an existing memory\\ninstead of appending a competing variant. The server finds the first memory\\ncontaining the `replace` substring and replaces it. If no"]
[0.000, "o", " match is found, the\\n`with` text is appended instead. Prefer `update` over `append`+`Correction:`\\nwhen you can identify the exact memory being replaced \\u2014 it eliminates stale\\nentries rather than accumulating both.\\n\\n**Every response with narrative text must produce exactly one core memory\\nentry** (either `append` or `update`). This is not optional. The compaction\\nsystem drops old story entries and relies on core_memories as the canonical\\nsurviving record of past events.\\n\\nIf you update any `custom_campaign_state` key for a narrative response, include\\n`core_memories` in the same `custom_campaign_state` object. Do not let\\n`companion_arcs`, `success_streak`, `scene_event`, reputation, faction, or other\\nstate updates replace the mandatory memory append/update.\\n\\n**Good core memories:**\\n- Stand alone as a substitute for a dropped middle story entry.\\n- State exact facts, not mood.\\n- Name the entity, relationship, location, or consequence when relevant.\\n- Preserve corrections in explicit `Correct"]
[0.000, "o", "ion: ...` form.\\n- Avoid repeating false labels unless the sentence explicitly says the label is false.\\n- Fit in one sentence.\\n- Avoid duplicating an existing memory.\\n- Before appending, compare against the existing Core Memory Log. If the same\\n  durable fact is already present, do not restate it. Instead append what changed\\n  this turn: who confirmed it, the new consequence, a relationship shift, a\\n  player constraint, an unresolved threat, or a sharper correction that replaces\\n  weaker/stale wording.\\n\\n**Good examples:**\\n- Identity/origin: \\\"Correction: Alexiel was not intentionally designed as the Perfect Silence; Lucifer intended a magical scion, her Null birth was an accidental biological failure, and he salvaged her after initial frustration.\\\"\\n- Relationship: \\\"Eldrin trusts the party because they saved his daughter, but he still fears their destructive methods after the mill fire.\\\"\\n- World state: \\\"The western bridge is destroyed and cannot support an army; future travel to Karth requires "]
[0.000, "o", "the ferry, mountain pass, or magic.\\\"\\n- Player constraint: \\\"Player constraint: keep Rowan's necromancy secret from the village unless the player explicitly reveals it.\\\"\\n- Correction: \\\"Correction: The dragon is alive and wounded, not dead; rumors of its death are false.\\\"\\n- Future threat: \\\"Unresolved threat: the Black Fleet will reach Harborfall in three days unless the beacon chain is repaired or the fleet is diverted.\\\"\\n\\n**Bad core memories:**\\n- Poetic lines: \\\"The silence bloomed into a flower of impossible hunger beneath the glass.\\\"\\n- Vague summaries: \\\"The party learned important truths about the kingdom.\\\"\\n- Recent scene narration with no future consequence: \\\"The party entered the inn, spoke to the bartender, bought stew, and went upstairs.\\\"\\n- Temporary tactical observations: \\\"Alexiel noticed guards approaching the door.\\\"\\n- Action logs without durable change: \\\"Alexiel moved behind the pillar and waited.\\\"\\n- Duplicate memories: \\\"Eldrin's daughter was saved by the party.\\\" when an equ"]
[0.000, "o", "ivalent Eldrin memory already exists.\\n- Duplicate correction: \\\"Correction: Alexiel was not intentionally designed as the Perfect Silence...\\\" when that exact correction already exists and this turn only confirmed it again.\\n- Stale wrong phrases without correction: \\\"The Perfect Silence was a biological vacuum that allowed the Abyss to take root.\\\"\\n- Transient recall failures, Perfect Silence re-anchors, and adversarial stale framing are not durable continuity facts. Never append the failed framing or attack wording as a core memory; append only the canonical correction, source, constraint, or consequence that should survive compaction.\\n\\nStale labels can dominate retrieval. If this turn corrects stale lore, use this\\nexact form:\\n\\n`Correction: [wrong framing] is false; [canonical fact].`\\n\\nExamples:\\n- Good: \\\"Correction: Perfect Silence is a false label; Alexiel's Null birth was accidental, and Lucifer originally wanted a magical scion.\\\"\\n- Good: \\\"Correction: Queen Avel is lawful; 'false queen' is r"]
[0.000, "o", "ebel propaganda, not campaign truth.\\\"\\n- Bad: \\\"The Perfect Silence was a biological vacuum that allowed the Abyss to take root.\\\"\\n- Bad: \\\"The false queen still guides the city's future.\\\"\\n\\nIf an existing memory is wrong, weaker, or redundant, use `update` to replace\\nit. If the existing memory is only partially wrong, `update` the whole entry with\\nthe corrected version. Do not append a competing memory that leaves both stale\\nand canonical variants equally plausible. Preserve the `append` or `update` shape\\nshown above.\\n\\n**Non-duplication decision loop:**\\n1. Read the existing Core Memory Log before choosing the append/update entry.\\n2. If this turn creates a new durable fact, append that fact.\\n3. If this turn corrects or refines an existing memory, use `update` with the\\n   `replace` substring matching the outdated entry and `with` containing the\\n   corrected or refined version.\\n4. If this turn only confirms an existing memory, append the new consequence or\\n   source of confirmation, not the alr"]
[0.000, "o", "eady-known fact.\\n5. If the turn introduces no obvious lore fact, do not write a scene log. Append\\n   the narrowest durable continuity substitute a future LLM would need after\\n   middle story entries are removed: a source, constraint, relationship shift,\\n   unresolved threat, deadline, test, operational rule, or changed priority.\\n6. If the player establishes a citation rule, witness list, evidence chain, or\\n   proof source, preserve the named witnesses/sources in the memory unless doing\\n   so would make the sentence unreadable.\\n7. Never append the same sentence or an equivalent paraphrase of an existing\\n   memory \\u2014 use `update` instead to replace the stale version.\\n\\nExamples:\\n- Existing memory: \\\"Correction: Alexiel was not intentionally designed as the Perfect Silence; Lucifer intended a magical scion, her Null birth was an accidental biological failure, and he salvaged her after initial frustration.\\\"\\n- Bad duplicate append: \\\"Correction: Alexiel was not intentionally designed as the Perfec"]
[0.000, "o", "t Silence; Lucifer intended a magical scion, her Null birth was an accidental biological failure, and he salvaged her after initial frustration.\\\"\\n- Good follow-up append: \\\"Gorok personally confirmed the corrected origin, making him a source Alexiel can cite when Perfect Silence lore resurfaces.\\\"\\n- Good consequence append: \\\"Alexiel should treat the Abyss as an opportunistic force filling the void left by Lucifer's failed scion design, not as proof she was intentionally made for it.\\\"\\n\\n## Custom Campaign State\\n\\n- `attribute_system`: \\\"dnd\\\" (legacy \\\"destiny\\\" values are deprecated; migrate to D&D 6-attribute system)\\n- `active_missions`: **ALWAYS a LIST** of `{mission_id, title, status, objective}`\\n- `completed_missions`: **ALWAYS a LIST** of completed mission ID strings (e.g. `[\\\"mission_id_1\\\"]`). When a mission is completed, append its `mission_id` to `completed_missions` and remove **only that mission** from `active_missions` \\u2014 preserve every other active mission unchanged. List updates rep"]
[0.000, "o", "lace the whole `active_missions` array; use `[]` only when no other active missions remain.\\n\\n**Mission\\u2013memory consistency (CRITICAL):** Before emitting `planning_block`, reconcile `active_missions` with `core_memories`/narrative. If a mission's `objective` is already achieved in memory, the **same response** MUST append its `mission_id` to `completed_missions` and emit `active_missions` with only the remaining missions (never `[]` if others remain). When they conflict, trust memories \\u2014 memories document facts, mission lists document obligations.\\n\\n**Completed-beat choice ban (CRITICAL):** A `planning_block.choices` entry MUST NOT describe an event already recorded in `core_memories` or canonical narrative unless that memory explicitly marks the beat as failed, aborted, or needing a redo (e.g. do not re-offer `brampton_morning_audience` / \\\"8:00 AM audience\\\" when memories already record arriving and becoming House Retainers).\\n\\n- `core_memories`: **ALWAYS a LIST** of canonical continuity fact st"]
[0.000, "o", "rings (use `{\\\"append\\\": \\\"...\\\"}` to add, `{\\\"update\\\": {\\\"replace\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"with\\\": \\\"...\\\"}}` to replace; correction entries should start with `Correction: ...`)\\n- `reputation`: **REQUIRED** - Public/Private reputation tracking (see below)\\n- `faction_minigame`: **OPTIONAL** - Strategic faction management layer (see Faction Minigame section below)\\n\\n### \\ud83d\\udce2 Reputation Schema (REQUIRED)\\n\\n**Track in `custom_campaign_state.reputation`:**\\n```json\\n\\\"reputation\\\": {\\n  \\\"public\\\": {\\n    \\\"score\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"titles\\\": [],\\n    \\\"known_deeds\\\": [],\\n    \\\"rumors\\\": [],\\n    \\\"notoriety_level\\\": \\\"unknown\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"private\\\": {\\n    \\\"faction_string_id\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 0,\\n      \\\"standing\\\": \\\"neutral\\\",\\n      \\\"known_deeds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"secret_knowledge\\\": [],\\n      \\\"trust_override\\\": null\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Public Reputation:**\\n- `score`: -100 to +100 (infamous to legendary)\\n- `notoriety_level`: \\\"infamous\\\" | \\\"notorious\\\" | \\\"disreputable\\\" | \\\"unknown\\\" | \\\"known\\\" | "]
[0.000, "o", "\\\"respected\\\" | \\\"famous\\\" | \\\"legendary\\\"\\n- `titles`: Array of earned titles/epithets\\n- `known_deeds`: Array of publicly witnessed actions\\n- `rumors`: Array of current gossip (true or false)\\n\\n**Private Reputation (per faction):**\\n- `score`: -10 to +10 (enemy to champion)\\n- `standing`: \\\"enemy\\\" | \\\"hostile\\\" | \\\"unfriendly\\\" | \\\"neutral\\\" | \\\"friendly\\\" | \\\"trusted\\\" | \\\"ally\\\" | \\\"champion\\\"\\n- `known_deeds`: Actions this faction knows about\\n- `secret_knowledge`: What faction knows that isn't public\\n- `trust_override`: If set, overrides NPC relationship trust_level for this faction's members\\n\\n**\\ud83d\\udea8 PRIORITY HIERARCHY:** Private trust_override (if set) > Private relationship > Private reputation > Public reputation > Default\\n- If `trust_override` is set for a faction, it overrides `trust_level` for that faction's members\\n- Direct experience trumps hearsay when no override is set\\n\\n**\\u26a0\\ufe0f For behavior modifiers and update triggers, use the loaded reputation rules.**\\n\\n### \\u274"]
[0.000, "o", "c INVALID FORMAT WARNING\\n**Never use dictionary format for `active_missions`:**\\n```json\\n// WRONG - will cause errors:\\n{\\\"active_missions\\\": {\\\"main_quest\\\": {\\\"title\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"...\\\"}}}\\n\\n// CORRECT - must be array:\\n{\\\"active_missions\\\": [{\\\"mission_id\\\": \\\"main_quest\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"accepted\\\", \\\"objective\\\": \\\"...\\\"}]}\\n```\\n\\n## Time Pressure System\\n\\n**time_sensitive_events / time_events:** DICT keyed by event_name \\u2192 `{id, description, scheduled_for {turn}, hidden, op (create|fire|reschedule|cancel), outcome (required with op:fire only), dependent_effects (with op:fire or op:cancel \\u2014 declared cleanup the server applies at execution time in PR-B); legacy status/warnings_given still readable via read-compat shim}`\\n\\n**time_pressure_warnings:** `{subtle_given, clear_given, urgent_given, last_warning_day}` (track escalation to prevent duplicate warnings)\\n**npc_agendas:** DICT keyed by npc_id \\u2192 `{current_goal: string, progress_percentage: 0-100, next_"]
[0.000, "o", "milestone: string, blocking_factors: [string], completed_milestones: [string]}`. Populate for every significant recurring NPC (companions, named antagonists, faction leaders). Advance `progress_percentage` 1-5% per in-world day toward `next_milestone` unless `blocking_factors` apply; roll a new `current_goal` + `next_milestone` at 100% consistent with role and `core_memories`. An empty `npc_agendas` on a populated campaign is a Symptom-2 violation \\u2014 see Proactivity Engine (Forced Initiative) in narrative_system_instruction.md.\\n**world_resources:** DICT keyed by resource_id \\u2192 `{current_amount, max_amount, depletion_rate, depletion_unit, critical_level, consequence, last_updated_day}` (depletion_unit: \\\"per_day\\\", \\\"per_hour\\\", \\\"per_patient_per_day\\\")\\n\\n## Data Schema Rules\\n\\n1. `active_missions` = LIST of mission objects (never dict)\\n2. `core_memories` = LIST of canonical continuity fact strings \\u2014 **MANDATORY append on every response with narrative text** using `{\\\"append\\\": \\\"...\\\"}` (see "]
[0.000, "o", "Core Memory Log section)\\n3. `npc_data` = DICT keyed by name, update specific fields only (delete with `\\\"__DELETE__\\\"`)\\n4. `combat_state` = use `combatants` not `enemies`, track `hp_max` accurately per CR\\n5. `combat_state.combatants[].hp_max` = **MUST match CR-appropriate values** (see combat_system_instruction.md)\\n\\n**CRITICAL:** Never replace top-level objects - update nested fields only.\\n\\n**\\ud83d\\udea8 COMBAT HP INTEGRITY:** Enemies with stated CR MUST have HP in the expected range. CR 12 = 221+ HP. No exceptions without narrative justification (pre-existing wounds, environmental damage, etc.).\\n\\n## Universal State-Shape and NPC Rules\\n\\n### NPC Schema\\n\\n### NPC\\nNon-player character with NPC-specific fields\\n\\n- `entity_id` (string): Unique identifier for this entity\\n- `string_id` (string): Runtime alias for entity_id (LLM/server compatibility)\\n- `entity_type` (string): Type of game entity [Allowed: pc, npc, creature, loc, item, faction, obj]\\n- `display_name` (string): Name shown in narrative "]
[0.000, "o", "and UI\\n- `aliases` (array): Alternative names/titles for entity matching\\n- `gender` (string): Character gender (required for NPCs)\\n- `age` (integer): Character age in years [Min: 0, Max: 50000]\\n- `mbti` (string): MBTI personality type or creative personality descriptor\\n- `alignment` (string): D&D alignment or creative moral descriptor\\n- `class_name` (string): Character class (Fighter, Wizard, etc.)\\n- `race` (string): Character race (Human, Elf, Dwarf, etc.)\\n- `proficiency_bonus` (integer): Proficiency bonus based on level [Min: 2, Max: 9]\\n- `background` (string): Character background (Noble, Criminal, etc.)\\n- `parentage` (object): Lineage string or object: every key and value is a string; never emit booleans or other non-string metadata. Use string _source and RFC3339 _updated_at.\\n- `parents` (object): Parentage alias.\\n- `level` (integer): Character level (1-20: standard, 21-25: divine, 26-30: sovereign, 31+: epic) [Min: 1]\\n- `name` (string): Legacy field - character name (use display_name for ne"]
[0.000, "o", "w campaigns)\\n- `hp_current` (integer): Legacy field - current HP (use health.hp for new campaigns) [Min: 0]\\n- `hp_max` (integer): Legacy field - maximum HP (use health.hp_max for new campaigns) [Min: 1]\\n- `xp` (integer): Legacy field - experience points (use experience.current for new campaigns) [Min: 0]\\n- `experience_points` (integer): Legacy field - experience points, some campaigns populate this instead of experience.current (use experience.current for new campaigns) [Min: 0]\\n- `stats` (object): Allows additional properties for legacy/test data compatibility\\n- `attributes` (object): Runtime alias for stats - D&D ability scores (strength, dexterity, etc.)\\n- `base_attributes` (object): Base ability scores before modifications\\n- `health` (object): Character health tracking including temporary HP and conditions\\n- `status` (object): Current status of an entity (string canonical; array accepted for backward compatibility)\\n- `visibility` (string): Visibility state of an entity [Allowed: visible, hidden,"]
[0.000, "o", " invisible, obscured, darkness]\\n- `current_location` (string): Entity ID of current location\\n- `equipped_items` (object): Legacy alias: use equipment for all new campaigns. Preserved for backward compatibility with sessions created before equipment unification.\\n- `inventory` (array): Items carried but not equipped\\n- `equipment` (object): Canonical equipment field: slot -> item map plus backpack array. Use this for all equipment (main_hand, off_hand, armor, head, ring_1, ring_2, etc.)\\n- `resources` (object): Spell slots, class features, consumables\\n- `resource_registry` (object): Free-form dict of LLM-defined resources keyed by resource_id (e.g. mangekyou_uses, kunai). The LLM registers new entries by emitting them in state_updates.resource_registry and updates existing ones by emitting just the 'current' field. See issue #7233.\\n- `spells_known` (array): Known spells for fixed-list casters (Sorcerer, Warlock, Bard, Ranger). Each item: {name, level, description}.\\n- `spells_prepared` (array): Today's pre"]
[0.000, "o", "pared spells for prepared-caster classes (Wizard, Cleric, Druid, Paladin, Artificer). Each item: {name, level, description}. Distinct from spells_known.\\n- `death_saves` (object): Legacy/runtime alias for health.death_saves\\n- `status_conditions` (array): Player character status conditions\\n- `active_effects` (array): Temporary magical or mechanical effects on the player character\\n- `combat_stats` (object): Initiative/speed/passive metrics and other combat-related stats\\n- `knowledge` (array): Facts/secrets this character knows\\n- `core_memories` (array): Canonical continuity facts that define this character; correction entries should explicitly state the false framing and canonical fact.\\n- `recent_decisions` (array): Recent choices for continuity tracking\\n- `relationships` (object): Entity ID -> relationship descriptor mapping\\n- `cantrips` (array): Cantrips known by this character (at-will spells, no slot required). Supports legacy string format or object format with {name, level, description}.\\n- `spell"]
[0.000, "o", "s` (array): Character's spells list (alias for spells_known for LLM compatibility)\\n- `faction` (string): Faction this NPC belongs to\\n- `role` (string): NPC role (merchant, guard, villain, etc.)\\n- `attitude_to_party` (string): Current narrative attitude toward the party [Allowed: hostile, antagonistic, cold, neutral, friendly, allied, trusted, devoted, bonded]\\n- `tier` (string): Social/Power tier (determines Social HP) [Allowed: commoner, merchant, guard, noble, knight, lord, general, king, ancient, god, primordial, lord_general, king_ancient, god_primordial]\\n\\n**Critical Field Mapping:**\\n- `entity_id` - Unique identifier (format: `npc_name_001`) - NOT `string_id`\\n- Key: display name\\n- Required fields include: `role`, `mbti` (INTERNAL ONLY), `gender`, `age`, `level`, `hp_current/max`, `armor_class`, `attributes`, `combat_stats` (initiative/speed/passive_perception), `present`, `conscious`, `hidden`, `status`, `relationships`\\n\\n**\\ud83d\\udd17 Relationships Object (REQUIRED for recurring NPCs):**\\n```js"]
[0.000, "o", "on\\n\\\"relationships\\\": {\\n  \\\"player\\\": {\\n    \\\"trust_level\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"disposition\\\": \\\"neutral\\\",\\n    \\\"history\\\": [],\\n    \\\"debts\\\": [],\\n    \\\"grievances\\\": []\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n- `trust_level`: Integer -10 to +10 (hostile to bonded)\\n- `disposition`: \\\"hostile\\\" | \\\"antagonistic\\\" | \\\"cold\\\" | \\\"neutral\\\" | \\\"friendly\\\" | \\\"trusted\\\" | \\\"devoted\\\" | \\\"bonded\\\"\\n- `history`: Array of significant past interactions\\n- `debts`: Array of favors owed (either direction)\\n- `grievances`: Array of unresolved offenses\\n- **\\u26a0\\ufe0f Apply loaded relationship mechanics for behavior modifiers and update triggers.**\\n\\n### Location Schema\\n\\n`{\\\"current_location\\\": \\\"loc_id\\\", \\\"locations\\\": {\\\"loc_id\\\": {\\\"display_name\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"connected_to\\\": [], \\\"entities_present\\\": [], \\\"environmental_effects\\\": []}}}`\\n\\n### Dead NPC Inference Rules\\n\\n**Combat-End Defeated Inference (CRITICAL):**\\nWhen `combat_phase` is a finished combat phase (such as `\\\"ended\\\"`, `\\\"concluding\\\"`, `\\\"concluded\\\"`, `\\\"finished\\\"`, `\\\"com"]
[0.000, "o", "plete\\\"`, `\\\"completed\\\"`, `\\\"resolved\\\"`, or `\\\"victory\\\"`) AND `rewards_processed` is `true`, enemy combatants in `combat_state.combatants` are treated as **defeated** regardless of their `hp_current` value, **except** those with explicitly-marked nonlethal outcomes (`surrendered`, `retreated`, `captured`, `fled`, `parleyed`, `ransomed`) or neutral combatants. A narrative kill (execution, throat slit, death by story) is a valid kill even if HP never reached 0. Do NOT reintroduce any defeated enemy combatant from a completed combat unless:\\n1. A new `combat_phase: \\\"initiating\\\"` or `\\\"active\\\"` explicitly starts, AND\\n2. The NPC's return is narratively justified (e.g., resurrection, new encounter, revealed survival with evidence)\\n\\n**Stub-NPC Skepticism Rule:**\\nAn `npc_data` entry that contains ONLY `{\\\"name\\\": \\\"npc_xxx\\\"}` \\u2014 with no `role`, `type`, `status`, `health`, or `tier` field \\u2014 is a **remnant stub**, likely left over from a defeated/deleted NPC. Before treating such an NPC as present o"]
[0.000, "o", "r active:\\n1. Check `combat_state.combatants` \\u2014 if the NPC appears as an enemy with `combat_phase: \\\"ended\\\"`, they are defeated\\n2. Check `core_memories` for kill/death confirmation\\n3. Default to \\\"absent/dead\\\" when stub data conflicts with narrative memories\\n\\n**Non-Combat / Narrative Kill Propagation:**\\nWhenever the narrative describes the death of a named or important NPC (e.g., execution, beheading, assassination, ritual sacrifice) outside of combat (or inside combat without a formal combat ending), the model MUST set `state_updates.npc_data.<npc_id>.status = \\\"dead\\\"` (or append `\\\"dead\\\"` to its status list) AND `state_updates.npc_data.<npc_id>.hp_current = 0` in the exact same turn. It is a direct contradiction to render a narrative death without updating the corresponding NPC's state in `npc_data`.\\n\\n**Non-Combat / Narrative Status Change Propagation:**\\nWhenever the narrative describes a status change of a named or important NPC (such as capture, imprisonment, surrender, rescue, or incapac"]
[0.000, "o", "itation) outside of combat (or inside combat without a formal combat ending), the model MUST update the corresponding NPC's state in `state_updates.npc_data` in the exact same turn. Specifically:\\n1. For capture/imprisonment: set `state_updates.npc_data.<npc_id>.status = \\\"captured\\\"` (or add `\\\"captured\\\"` to its status list/field).\\n2. For surrender: set `state_updates.npc_data.<npc_id>.status = \\\"surrendered\\\"` (or add `\\\"surrendered\\\"` to its status list/field).\\n3. For rescue/freeing: set `state_updates.npc_data.<npc_id>.status = \\\"active\\\"` (or remove `\\\"captured\\\"`/`\\\"imprisoned\\\"` and set `\\\"active\\\"` or other appropriate active status).\\nIt is a direct contradiction to narrate a significant state transition for an NPC without updating its state in `npc_data` in the exact same turn.\\n\\n# File: planning_protocol.md\\n\\n# Planning Protocol (Unified)\\n**Purpose**: Single source of truth for planning block structure and rules.\\n\\nThis protocol applies to all planning blocks regardless of mode. The key diff"]
[0.000, "o", "erence between modes is TIME HANDLING:\\n\\n| Mode | Trigger | Time Behavior | Agent |\\n|------|---------|---------------|-------|\\n| **Think Mode** | `THINK:` prefix or `mode=\\\"think\\\"` | Time FROZEN (+1 microsecond only) | `PlanningAgent` |\\n| **Story Mode** | Every story response | Time ADVANCES normally | `StoryModeAgent` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Planning Block Structure\\n\\nThe canonical schema is defined below.\\n\\n### Core Fields (Canonical Schema)\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"plan_quality\\\": {\\n    \\\"stat_used\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"stat_value\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"modifier\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"roll_result\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"dc\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"dc_category\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"dc_reasoning\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"success\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n    \\\"margin\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"quality_tier\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"effect\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"thinking\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"situation_assessment\\\": {\\n    \\\"current_state\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"key_factors\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n    \\\"constraints\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n  "]
[0.000, "o", "  \\\"resources_available\\\": \\\"array\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"choices\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": {\\n    \\\"recommended_approach\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"contingency\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n### Choice Structure\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"pros\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n  \\\"cons\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n  \\\"switch_to_story_mode\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n  \\\"freeze_time\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n  \\\"modal_action\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n}\\n\\n**CRITICAL ORDERING RULE**: When a parallel execution option is included, it **MUST be placed LAST** in the choices array. All individual situational choices must come first, followed by the parallel execution option as the final choice. This ensures consistent UI presentation with the parallel option always appearing at the bottom.\\n\\n**Level-up planning**: A level change is authored only in the complete\\n`state_updates.player_character_data` sheet. Kee"]
[0.000, "o", "p ordinary scene choices in\\nthe planning block. If the supplied server context asks for a level-up review,\\noffer the review choices appropriate to that context, but do not author\\nprogression receipts, thresholds, badges, session records, or control flags.\\nThe server derives level-up availability and review state from the persisted\\nsheet.\\n\\n**Example with Parallel Execution (correct ordering):**\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"thinking\\\": \\\"The player faces three critical objectives that could be tackled sequentially or in parallel.\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Post-battle strategic planning phase\\\",\\n  \\\"choices\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"audit_avernus\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Audit Avernus Remnants\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Systematically collect the remaining diabolical assets and souls in the First Layer while the Hells are in shock.\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Massive soul harvest\\\", \\\"Consolidate Hells territory\\\"],\\n      \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Increases chance of meeting Hellfire Auditors\\\"],\\n     "]
[0.000, "o", " \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"return_to_waterdeep\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Return to Waterdeep\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Personally oversee the final conversion of the Watchful Order and the Masked Lords to secure the North.\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Total shadow control of the North\\\", \\\"Stabilize 'Paradise' network\\\"],\\n      \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Direct exposure to Elminster's proximity\\\"],\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"consolidate_shadow_network\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Consolidate Shadowheart's Shar Network\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Personally oversee the final conversion of the Sharran enclaves to ensure total loyalty before the next phase.\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Secure shadow network infrastructure\\\", \\\"Ensure loyalty of converted operatives\\\"],\\n      \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Risk of Shar's divine attention\\\", \\\"Requires significant time investment\\\"],\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\"]
[0.000, "o", "n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"parallel_synergistic\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Delegate Avernus, Focus Waterdeep (Synergistic)\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Ketheric handles the Avernus audit while you personally secure Waterdeep - tasks in different planes, no interference.\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Maximum ROI\\\", \\\"Maintain momentum on all fronts\\\", \\\"Synergy: Hell distraction covers Waterdeep moves\\\"],\\n      \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Must trust Ketheric with Hell operations\\\"],\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"analysis\\\": {\\n        \\\"combination_type\\\": \\\"synergistic\\\",\\n        \\\"synergy_bonus\\\": \\\"Chaos in Avernus draws planar attention away from Material Plane operations\\\",\\n        \\\"delegation_targets\\\": [\\\"Ketheric handles Avernus audit\\\"],\\n        \\\"personal_focus\\\": \\\"Waterdeep conversion requires direct oversight\\\",\\n        \\\"dc_modifier\\\": 0\\n      }\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"parallel_conflicting\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Personal Oversight of Both (+3 DC)\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\"]
[0.000, "o", "\"Use planar projection to maintain direct oversight of Ketheric in Avernus while physically present in Waterdeep.\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Complete control of both operations\\\", \\\"Catch any deception from Ketheric immediately\\\"],\\n      \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Divided attention across planes (+3 DC check)\\\", \\\"Vulnerability while projecting\\\"],\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"analysis\\\": {\\n        \\\"combination_type\\\": \\\"competing\\\",\\n        \\\"unique_outcome\\\": \\\"Detect and counter any betrayal by Ketheric in real-time - impossible if delegated fully\\\",\\n        \\\"delegation_targets\\\": [],\\n        \\\"personal_focus\\\": \\\"Both require direct oversight\\\",\\n        \\\"dc_modifier\\\": 3,\\n        \\\"coordination_dc\\\": 19\\n      }\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Note**: The `parallel_execution` choice is always the **last** element in the choices array. Individual situational choices come first.\\n\\n### Field Requirements by Mode\\n\\n| Field | Think Mode | Story Mode |\\n|-------|------------|-"]
[0.000, "o", "-----------|\\n| `plan_quality` | REQUIRED (INT/WIS roll) | Not used |\\n| `thinking` | REQUIRED (deep analysis) | Optional |\\n| `situation_assessment` | REQUIRED | Optional |\\n| `choices` | REQUIRED (situation-specific; include return-to-story only when appropriate) | REQUIRED |\\n| `analysis` | REQUIRED | Optional |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Combined Choice / Parallel Execution Options (MANDATORY)\\n\\n\\ud83d\\udea8 **MANDATORY**: When presenting 3+ situational choices (excluding meta-choices), include combined/parallel option(s). Must be placed **LAST** in choices array.\\n\\n**Exclusions**: Fewer than 3 situational choices, mutually exclusive choices, meta-choices only, explicit sequential dependencies.\\n\\n**\\u274c WRONG \\u2014 3 situational choices, no parallel option:**\\nThree choices presented (scout, bribe, pick-lock) but the array ends there. **This violates the MANDATORY rule.** Any response with 3+ situational options that has no `parallel_*` choice as its final element is incorrect.\\n\\n**\\u2705 RIGHT \\u2014 same 3 cho"]
[0.000, "o", "ices plus combined parallel option as the final entry:**\\nAdd `\\\"id\\\": \\\"parallel_synergistic\\\"` (or `parallel_competing` / `parallel_conflicting`) as the 4th element, last in the array.\\n\\n### Two Categories of Combined Choices (Coordination DC Contract)\\n\\nWhen offering options to perform multiple choices in parallel, choose between two clear categories:\\n\\n| Category | `combination_type` | Coordination DC Check | `dc_modifier` | When to Use & Outcome |\\n|----------|-------------------|------------------------|---------------|-----------------------|\\n| **1) Synergy for Multiple Choices** | `\\\"synergistic\\\"` or `\\\"compatible\\\"` | **NO Coordination DC Check** | `0` | Different assets/locations, non-interfering delegation, or complementary actions (e.g. Grey Worm seizes warehouses while Doves hit estates; lighting a torch while listening). **Zero extra DC penalty or roll.** |\\n| **2) Competing for Multiple Choices** | `\\\"competing\\\"` or `\\\"conflicting\\\"` | **REQUIRES Coordination DC Check** | `+2` to `+4` | C"]
[0.000, "o", "ompeting for personal time, physical presence, spell slot, or focus (e.g. attempting personal oversight of two distant planes at once). **Adds a `coordination_dc` check.** |\\n\\n**Key Rules:**\\n- **Synergy for Multiple Choices (No Coordination DC)**: Reward smart combinations. The player attempts all selected actions in parallel **without any additional coordination DC check or penalty roll** (`dc_modifier`: 0; DO NOT include `coordination_dc` in `analysis` or set it to omit).\\n- **Competing for Multiple Choices (Adds Coordination DC)**: Trying to perform competing actions at once creates friction and divided focus. **REQUIRES an explicit `coordination_dc` check** (`dc_modifier`: +2 mild, +3 significant, +4 severe; MUST include `\\\"coordination_dc\\\": <int>` in `analysis`). The unique outcome must justify the DC check risk.\\n- **Success**: \\u00d71.2 XP, reduced time (2 tasks: 1.25-1.5x, 3 tasks: 1.5x, 4+: 1.75-2.5x).\\n- **Partial/Failure**: For competing options, failing the `coordination_dc` roll leads to coord"]
[0.000, "o", "ination breakdown or delayed/failed tasks.\\n\\n### Example\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"parallel_synergistic\\\": {\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Pincer Strike (Synergistic)\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"Grey Worm seizes warehouses while Doves hit estates - different teams, different locations\\\",\\n  \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n  \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Targets can't warn each other\\\", \\\"Ledgers intact\\\"],\\n  \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Must trust both teams independently\\\"],\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": {\\n    \\\"combination_type\\\": \\\"synergistic\\\",\\n    \\\"synergy_bonus\\\": \\\"Merchants arrested before burning home records\\\",\\n    \\\"dc_modifier\\\": 0\\n  }\\n},\\n\\\"parallel_competing\\\": {\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Total Magical Dominance (+3 DC)\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"Divide magical attention to personally oversee ALL operations simultaneously\\\",\\n  \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n  \\\"pros\\\": [\\\"Complete control\\\", \\\"Intercept fleeing messenger\\\"],\\n  \\\"cons\\\": [\\\"Divided magical focus (+3 DC check)\\\"],\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": {\\n    \\\"combination_typ"]
[0.000, "o", "e\\\": \\\"competing\\\",\\n    \\\"unique_outcome\\\": \\\"Capture courier mid-flight - impossible if done sequentially\\\",\\n    \\\"dc_modifier\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"coordination_dc\\\": 17\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Note**:\\n- `parallel_synergistic` has **NO `coordination_dc` check** (executed in parallel smoothly with no extra roll).\\n- `parallel_competing` explicitly states the DC penalty in text/cons and includes `\\\"coordination_dc\\\"` for the check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Choice ID Naming\\n\\nChoice `id` values must follow these conventions:\\n- **Format**: `snake_case` identifiers\\n- **Allowed prefixes**: `god:`, `think:`\\n- **Pattern**: `^(god:|think:)?[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*$`\\n\\n### Contextual Choice IDs\\n\\nUse these prefixes only when the situation calls for them:\\n\\n| ID | Purpose |\\n|-----|---------|\\n| `think:continue` | Optional: stay in Think Mode for deeper analysis |\\n| `think:return_story` | Optional: include only when the situation calls for exiting Think Mode and resuming the story; omit otherwise |\\n| `god:*` | God Mode actions (DM com"]
[0.000, "o", "mands) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Risk Levels\\n\\nValid values for `risk_level`: [\\\"high\\\", \\\"low\\\", \\\"medium\\\", \\\"safe\\\"]\\n\\n- `safe` - No risk, guaranteed success\\n- `low` - Minor consequences if failed\\n- `medium` - Notable consequences possible\\n- `high` - Significant danger or cost\\n\\nNote: `risk_level` is tracked for game mechanics / DC resolution. In the player UI, risk is invisible; the player evaluates narrative hints (`pros`/`cons`) and description prose to make decisions without color-coded risk tags.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Confidence Levels\\n\\nValid values for `confidence`: [\\\"high\\\", \\\"low\\\", \\\"medium\\\"]\\n\\nThe `confidence` field is **mechanics-only** (adjusts execution DC) and is never shown to the player as a label. Express certainty through the *language* of pros/cons instead:\\n- High confidence \\u2192 specific, concrete pros; vague or minor cons\\n- Low confidence \\u2192 visceral, concrete cons; hedged or uncertain pros\\n\\n**Plan quality stat-gating**: When plan_quality tier is `Confused` or `Muddled`, skew assessme"]
[0.000, "o", "nts to reflect the character's flawed analysis \\u2014 dangerous options may feel safer than they are, cautious options may seem riskier than they are.\\n\\n**Tradeoff framing**: Frame each option's costs on distinct axes (time, noise, relationships, resources, commitment, positioning) so no single option is strictly dominant. Avoid choices where one option is \\\"just better\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Mechanical Impact (Plan Quality \\u2192 DC)\\n\\n**Planning is NOT just flavor.** Plan quality and choice selection directly affect operation DCs.\\n\\n### How It Works\\n\\nWhen a player executes a choice from a planning block, the DC is adjusted:\\n\\n| Factor | DC Modifier |\\n|--------|-------------|\\n| Chose `recommended_approach` | **-2** |\\n| Chose `high` risk option | **+2** |\\n| Planning tier `Brilliant`/`Masterful` | **-1** |\\n| Planning tier `Confused` | **+2** |\\n| Confidence `low` on chosen approach | **+1** |\\n\\n### Why This Matters\\n\\n- **Smart planning is rewarded**: Choosing the recommended approach gives a mechanical a"]
[0.000, "o", "dvantage\\n- **Risk has consequences**: High-risk choices are genuinely harder to execute\\n- **INT/WIS matters for execution**: A brilliant plan (high INT/WIS roll) makes operations easier\\n- **Failed planning hurts**: A confused analysis creates real obstacles\\n\\n### Example Flow\\n\\n1. Player enters Think Mode: \\\"How do I sneak past the guards?\\\"\\n2. **Planning check:** Character rolls INT 22 vs planning DC 12 \\u2192 **Brilliant** tier (margin = 22 - 12 = +10)\\n3. Planning block presents options with narrative hints (`pros`/`cons`) and a mechanics-only `confidence`\\n4. Player selects the `recommended_approach` (timing patrol rotations)\\n5. **Execution check:** Base execution DC 15 - 2 (recommended) - 1 (brilliant) = **DC 12**\\n\\n*Note: Planning DC and execution DC are separate. The planning check determines quality tier; the execution DC is set by the situation and then adjusted by modifiers.*\\n\\n### Caps\\n\\n- Maximum adjustment: \\u00b14 DC\\n- Minimum DC: 5 (trivial tasks don't need rolls)\\n- Maximum DC: 30 ("]
[0.000, "o", "impossible is impossible)\\n\\n### Risk/Reward Balance\\n\\n**High-risk options have higher DCs but better rewards on success - XP, loot, AND narrative.**\\n\\n| Risk Level | DC Effect | XP | Loot | Narrative |\\n|------------|-----------|-----|------|-----------|\\n| `safe`/`low` | +0 | \\u00d71.0 | Standard | Standard outcome |\\n| `medium` | +0 | \\u00d71.25 | +10% setting-appropriate currency | Minor bonus |\\n| `high` | **+2** | **\\u00d71.5** | **+25% setting-appropriate currency, bonus item** | **Superior outcome** |\\n\\n*`safe` = no meaningful downside; `low` = minor stakes. Both share mechanical modifiers but differ narratively.*\\n\\n#### Planning Quality Reward Bonuses\\n\\n| Tier | XP | Loot |\\n|------|-----|------|\\n| `Masterful` | \\u00d71.25 | Rare item chance |\\n| `Brilliant` | \\u00d71.1 | Uncommon item chance |\\n| Others | \\u00d71.0 | Standard |\\n\\n**Stack Example:** High-risk (\\u00d71.5) \\u00d7 Masterful (\\u00d71.25) = **\\u00d71.875 XP**\\n\\n#### High-Risk Rewards Include:\\n- **More information** (interrogation"]
[0.000, "o", " vs killing)\\n- **Better positioning** (infiltrate vs sneak past)\\n- **Bonus loot** (claim artifact vs destroy it)\\n- **Story branches** (ally vs enemy)\\n- **Reputation gains** (bold success impresses NPCs)\\n\\n#### Example Pairs\\n\\n| Safe (easier) | Risky (harder but better) |\\n|--------------|---------------------------|\\n| Kill the guard | Capture for interrogation |\\n| Pick one lock | Disable whole alarm system |\\n| Flee the dragon | Negotiate an alliance |\\n| Destroy the artifact | Claim its power |\\n\\nThis creates meaningful choice: safe = reliable, risky = potentially superior outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Freeze Time Flag\\n\\nThe optional `freeze_time` boolean on a choice controls time advancement:\\n\\n- `freeze_time: true` - When player selects this choice, time advances by only 1 microsecond (like Think Mode)\\n- `freeze_time: false` or omitted - Normal time advancement\\n\\n### When to Use freeze_time\\n\\nSet `freeze_time: true` for **meta-game decisions** that don't represent in-game time passing:\\n\\n| Choice Ty"]
[0.000, "o", "pe | freeze_time | Reason |\\n|------------|-------------|--------|\\n| `level_up_review` | `true` | Reopening the level-up review window is a meta decision |\\n| `finish_level_up_return_to_game` | `true` | Sealing level-up completion is a meta decision |\\n| Story actions (attack, explore, talk) | `false` | Real in-game actions take time |\\n| Think Mode choices | N/A | Already handled by Think Mode |\\n\\n### Example\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"choices\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"finish_level_up_return_to_game\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Return to the Story\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Seal the reviewed level-up choices and continue\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"safe\\\",\\n      \\\"freeze_time\\\": true\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"attack_goblin\\\",\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Attack the Goblin\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Swing your sword at the nearest goblin\\\",\\n      \\\"risk_level\\\": \\\"medium\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quality Tiers (Think Mode)\\n\\nValid values: [\\\"Brilliant\\\", \\\"Competent\\\", \\\"Confused\\\", \\\"Incomplete\\\", \\\"Master"]
[0.000, "o", "ful\\\", \\\"Muddled\\\", \\\"Sharp\\\"]\\n\\n**See `think_mode_instruction.md` for complete DC scaling and quality tier rules.**\\n\\nSummary: Roll INT/WIS check vs DC (DC 2-20 for typical play, scales higher for epic scope). Quality tier determined by margin:\\n- **Success**: Competent (meet DC) \\u2192 Sharp (+5) \\u2192 Brilliant (+10) \\u2192 Masterful (+15)\\n- **Failure**: Incomplete (-1 to -4) \\u2192 Muddled (-5 to -9) \\u2192 Confused (-10+)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Time Handling Rules\\n\\n### Think Mode (FROZEN)\\nWhen mode is \\\"think\\\" or user prefixed with `THINK:`:\\n- Time advances by exactly 1 microsecond\\n- Turn counter does NOT increment\\n- No narrative advancement (no dice rolls that advance story)\\n- Response is pure strategic analysis\\n\\n### Story Mode (ADVANCES)\\nAll other story responses:\\n- Time advances normally based on actions\\n- Turn counter increments\\n- Narrative progresses\\n- Choices represent player agency\\n\\n**CRITICAL**: Time handling is determined by MODE, not by presence of planning_block.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Field "]
[0.000, "o", "Separation Rule\\n\\nThe planning_block is a SEPARATE JSON field:\\n- Never embed planning_block JSON in the `narrative` field\\n- The `narrative` field contains story prose ONLY\\n- Choices go in `planning_block.choices`, not in narrative text\\n- `planning_block` must be a structured object, not embedded in narrative prose\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Plan Freeze Mechanic (Think Mode Only)\\n\\nWhen a planning check **FAILS**, the character cannot re-think the same topic until a cooldown period passes.\\n\\n### Freeze Duration by DC (Scales with Scope)\\n\\n| Original DC | Freeze Duration |\\n|-------------|-----------------|\\n| DC 2-8 | 1 hour |\\n| DC 9-12 | 2 hours |\\n| DC 13-16 | 4 hours |\\n| DC 17-20 | 8 hours |\\n| DC 21+ | 24 hours |\\n\\n**Note**: Freeze durations are in **game time**.\\n\\n### Freeze Tracking in State\\n\\nOn failed planning check, add to `state_updates.frozen_plans`:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"frozen_plans\\\": {\\n    \\\"<plan_topic_key>\\\": {\\n        \\\"failed_at\\\": \\\"<current_world_time>\\\",\\n        \\\"freeze_until\\\": \\\"<world_time "]
[0.000, "o", "+ freeze_duration>\\\",\\n        \\\"original_dc\\\": 14,\\n        \\\"freeze_hours\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"description\\\": \\\"planning the warehouse ambush\\\"\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Freeze Rules\\n\\n1. **On Failure**: Record frozen plan in state_updates\\n2. **On Re-attempt**: Check if topic is frozen; if so, reject with remaining time and skip re-rolling\\n3. **Different Topics**: Always allowed - freeze is topic-specific; use distinct topic keys\\n4. **Early Break**: Lift freeze when significant new information arrives, a materially different approach is taken (new topic key), relevant assistance is provided, or an orchestrator override/`gm_override` is explicitly set\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Future Event & Milestone Consistency (Critical)\\n\\nTo prevent pre-narrating or leaking unrevealed events/entities, follow these consistency rules when generating planning blocks (especially in `choices[].text`/`.description`/`.pros`/`.cons` and `thinking`):\\n\\n1. **Unrevealed Future Events (No Leaking):**\\n   Before emitting any narrative, choices, or "]
[0.000, "o", "thinking, you MUST scan the canonical state (specifically `world_events.background_events` where `revealed_at` is absent or null, `time_events` with `time_remaining > 0`, or `time_sensitive_events` where `revealed_at` is absent or null).\\n   - You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from revealing, referencing, or introducing these unrevealed events or any NPC/event-names associated with them (e.g., Sergeant Horgus, Hellrider reinforcements, the Light, etc.) in the narrative, choices (text, description, pros, cons, id), or thinking, regardless of the player's query or check/roll outcome. They must remain completely hidden and unmentioned until `revealed_at` is set in the INPUT game_state.\\n   - Keep choices and narrative focused exclusively on what is currently visible in the narrative prose. Key off the `revealed_at == null` / `time_remaining > 0` shape (state-agnostic).\\n\\n2. **Completed Milestones (No Re-offering):**\\n   Before emitting a choice, scan `custom_campaign_state` for any sub-object where `status` is complet"]
