[0.000, "o", "@@ -1,202 +0,0 @@\r\n-\"\"\"Contract tests: level-up applies on the XP-crossing turn, with no button.\r\n-\r\n-Governing spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-26-automatic-level-up-crossing-turn-design.md\r\n-\r\n-The operator's verdict on the prior build:\r\n-\r\n-    \"if its auto apply but i still need to click to level up, it seems\r\n-    almost the same\"\r\n-    \"right now i keep seeing a button that says click to level up making\r\n-    me doubt it auto applied\"\r\n-\r\n-Measured locally: 750 XP, 0 to next level, character still Level 1 while a\r\n-\"Level Up to Level <N>\" button was shown in `planning_block.choices`. The\r\n-prompt as written mandated exactly that: a \"first turn\" that only *offers*\r\n-`level_up_now` as a clickable choice, followed by a separate \"grant turn\"\r\n-that applies the sheet only once the player clicks it\r\n-(`level_up_instruction.md` decision tree: \"first turn ... present\r\n-level_up_now choice\" / \"grant turn (user chose level_up_now)\").\r\n-\r\n-The fix is prompt-first: collapse the two-turn model into one. The mome"]
[0.000, "o", "nt\r\n-`target_level > current_level` is detected, the complete sheet must apply\r\n-in that same turn -- there is no entry-offer turn, and no `level_up_now`\r\n-choice is ever shown to the player.\r\n-\r\n-These tests assert the prompt STATES this contract. They are necessary but\r\n-not sufficient: only a real-LLM run (testing_mcp/core/test_level_up_organic.py\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "---level-up-scenario auto-apply-grant) proves the model actually obeys it.\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "-\"\"\"\r\n-\r\n"]
